e_end(toks)?;\n00177|         (action_end <= clause_start && positions_share_statement(request, action_end, clause_start))\n00178|             .then(|| request.get(action_end..clause_start))?\n00179|     } else if cue_is_destination {\n00180|         // The same shape read from the other side. A language that marks its\n00181|         // destination with a postposition and closes the clause with the verb —\n00182|         // \"notes/attribution.md में Gemfile.lock लिखो\" — states the payload\n00183|         // between the two, exactly as the prepositional wording states it\n00184|         // between the verb and the preposition.\n00185|         let action_start = action_cue_start_after(toks, binding.cue_end)?;\n00186|         (binding.cue_end <= action_start\n00187|             && positions_share_statement(request, binding.cue_end, action_start))\n00188|         .then(|| request.get(binding.cue_end..action_start))?\n00189|     } else if let Some(value_lead) = toks\n00190|         .iter()\n00191|         .skip(file_index + 1)\n00192|         .find(|token| dest_cues.contains(&clean_cue_token(token.text)))\n00193|     {\n00194|         // Assignment shape: \"set the contents of FILE to VALUE\". The target\n00195|         // cue identifies the file object and a following destination cue\n00196|         // introduces its literal value. Requiring a write action before the\n00197|         // file keeps an unrelated \"contents of FILE\" read request out.\n00198|         let action_end = first_action_cue_end(toks)?;\n00199|         (action_end <= clause_start\n00200|             && positions_share_statement(request, action_end, clause_start)\n00201|             && positions_share_statement(request, clause_start, value_lead.start))\n00202|         .then(|| request.get(value_lead.end..))?\n00203|     } else {\n00204|         None\n00205|     };\n00206|     let content = clean_content(content_span?)?;\n00207|     // A recovered payload that is *only* a non-referential subject (\"save it to\n00208|     // FILE\", \"write this to FILE\") names no literal content — the pronoun points\n00209|     // back at content the request expects the recipe to still compose. Treating\n00210|     // it as a literal write both fabricates the wrong file (the string \"it\") and\n00211|     // steals the request from the keyword recipe that would author the real\n00212|     // artifact, so fall through instead (issue #663).\n00213|     //\n00214|     // The same is true of a payload that names the *work product* rather than\n00215|     // supplying it: \"save the answer to FILE\" states where an answer goes, not\n00216|     // what it says (issue #1066).\n00217|     if is_non_referential_content(&content)\n00218|         || names_deferred_work_product(&content)\n00219|         || !is_literal_content(&content, content_span, false)\n00220|     {\n00221|         return None;\n00222|     }\n00223|     Some((target, content))\n00224| }\n00225| /// Mask literal punctuation without changing UTF-8 byte positions.\n00226| fn statement_scope(request: &str) -> String {\n00227|     let mut scoped = request.to_owned();\n00228|     for segment in crate::normal_markov::quoted_segment_spans(request) {\n00229|         scoped.replace_range(\n00230|             segment.start..segment.end,\n00231|             &\" \".repeat(segment.end - segment.start),\n00232|         );\n00233|     }\n00234|     scoped\n00235| }\n00236| /// Where the statement that begins at `from` ends, never past `limit`.\n00237| ///\n00238| /// A literal payload is something the request *states*, and a statement ends\n00239| /// where its sentence does. Bounding the span by the file clause alone reads\n00240| /// across every sentence in between: \"Draft a handover memo containing the\n00241| /// migration status, the outstanding blockers, and the on-call owner. Leave the\n00242| /// memo in `handover/2026-q3.md`\" put the marker in the first sentence and the\n00243| /// clause in the second, so the recovered payload ended with the words *Leave\n00244| /// the memo* and the caller's memo opened by instructing them to leave it\n00245| /// (issue #1066).\n00246| ///\n00247| /// The clause bound still applies inside the sentence, because \"write the\n00248| /// following: hello to `x.txt`\" states marker, payload and clause in one\n00249| /// breath. This only refuses to look further than the sentence the marker is in.\n00250| ///\n00251| /// A marker that says nothing more on its own line is the exception, because\n00252| /// there the payload is a *block* rather than a phrase: \"Create file\n00253| /// `rules.lino` containing\\n<three lines of lino>\" leaves the marker with an\n00254| /// empty tail, and a newline ends a sentence, so the sentence bound would\n00255| /// recover nothing at all. When the marker's own line has no word left on it,\n00256| /// the statement is the block that follows and runs to `limit`, which is what\n00257| /// this route always did for block payloads.\n00258| ///\n00259| /// The sentence is the one *prose* reads, so a semicolon inside the payload\n00260| /// joins its two halves instead of ending it. Reading the payload at the scope\n00261| /// shell routing reads at cut issue #918's minimal-core invariant in half at\n00262| /// its semicolon.\n00263| fn end_of_statement(request: &str, from: usize, limit: usize) -> usize {\n00264|     let scoped = statement_scope(request);\n00265|     let Some(sentence) = prose_sentences(&scoped)\n00266|         .into_iter()\n00267|         .find(|sentence| sentence.span.contains(&from))\n00268|     else {\n00269|         return limit;\n00270|     };\n00271|     let says_more = request\n00272|         .get(from..sentence.span.end)\n00273|         .is_some_and(|tail| tail.chars().any(char::is_alphanumeric));\n00274|     if says_more && !payload_continues_past_its_first_line(request, from, sentence.span.end) {\n00275|         literal_statement_end(request, sentence.span.end).min(limit)\n00276|     } else {\n00277|         limit\n00278|     }\n00279| }\n00280| /// Whether two write-request cues belong to one prose statement.\n00281| ///\n00282| /// Literal-write roles are structural only inside the statement that relates\n00283| /// them. Without this check, a content lead in one issue-description paragraph\n00284| /// can pair with an incidental file-shaped token in a later paragraph and turn\n00285| /// repository policy prose into an executable write (issue #1069).\n00286| ///\n00287| /// [`end_of_statement`] deliberately lets a marker-only line introduce the\n00288| /// block below it. Reusing that boundary here preserves that supported block\n00289| /// shape while rejecting ordinary completed sentences between the two cues.\n00290| fn literal_statement_end(request: &str, from: usize) -> usize {\n00291|     let mut end = from;\n00292|     for character in request.get(from..).unwrap_or_default().chars() {\n00293|         if !matches!(character, '.' | '!' | '?' | '。' | '！' | '？' | '।') {\n00294|             break;\n00295|         }\n00296|         end += character.len_utf8();\n00297|     }\n00298|     end\n00299| }\n00300| fn positions_share_statement(request: &str, left: usize, right: usize) -> bool {\n00301|     let (from, limit) = if left <= right {\n00302|         (left, right)\n00303|     } else {\n00304|         (right, left)\n00305|     };\n00306|     from == limit || end_of_statement(request, from, limit) == limit\n00307| }\n00308| /// Punctuation needs a closed literal operand or a seeded explicit qualifier.\n00309| fn is_literal_content(content: &str, raw: Option<&str>, explicitly_qualified: bool) -> bool {\n00310|     if content.chars().any(char::is_alphanumeric) {\n00311|         return true;\n00312|     }\n00313|     let Some(raw) = raw.filter(|_| !content.is_empty()) else {\n00314|         return false;\n00315|     };\n00316|     let quoted = crate::normal_markov::quoted_segment_spans(raw);\n00317|     if let [only] = quoted.as_slice()\n00318|         && only.text == content\n00319|         && raw[..only.start]\n00320|             .chars()\n00321|             .all(|character| character.is_whitespace() || \":—–-\".contains(character))\n00322|         && raw[only.end..]\n00323|             .chars()\n00324|             .all(|character| character.is_whitespace() || \".!?。！？।\".contains(character))\n00325|     {\n00326|         return true;\n00327|     }\n00328|     explicitly_qualified\n00329|         && !content\n00330|             .chars()\n00331|             .any(|character| \"`\\\"'«»“”‘’„‚「」『』\".contains(character))\n00332| }\n00333| /// Whether a recovered write payload is nothing but a non-referential subject —\n00334| /// a bare pronoun/function word (\"it\", \"this\", \"that\", …) that refers back to\n00335| /// context rather than naming literal content. The surfaces carry the\n00336| /// [`seed::ROLE_NON_REFERENTIAL_SUBJECT`] role; only whole-word\n00337| /// ([`Slot::Bare`]) forms are rejected, so legitimate content that merely\n00338| /// *begins* with such a word (\"to be or not to be\") is still accepted.\n00339| fn is_non_referential_content(content: &str) -> bool {\n00340|     let lower = content.to_lowercase();\n00341|     seed::lexicon()\n00342|         .role_word_forms(seed::ROLE_NON_REFERENTIAL_SUBJECT)\n00343|         .iter()\n00344|         .any(|form| form.slot() == Slot::Bare && lower == form.text)\n00345| }\n00346| /// Whether a recovered write payload names the result of work the same request\n00347| /// asks for, instead of supplying bytes (issue #1066).\n00348| ///\n00349| /// \"Save the answer to `out/e.md`\" and \"leave observable evidence to\n00350| /// `out/e.md`\" have the destination-led shape of a literal write -- a write\n00351| /// verb, a span, a destination cue, a path -- and supply no literal. Taking the\n00352| /// span at face value wrote the words *the answer* into the file and, worse,\n00353| /// claimed the request as finished, so the investigation that would have\n00354| /// produced the real bytes never ran.\n00355| ///\n00356| /// The surfaces carry [`seed::ROLE_FILE_WRITE_DEFERRED_CONTENT_REFERENCE`] as\n00357| /// [`Slot::Suffix`] forms, because the head noun is what defers and the\n00358| /// modifiers in front of it (\"observable\", \"final\") are the caller's, not the\n00359| /// lexicon's.\n00360| ///\n00361| /// Applied to marker-led content too: “with the observed result” uses a content\n00362| /// marker syntactically, but still names work that has not happened. Explicit\n00363| /// bytes remain expressible through the authoritative-content marker, whose\n00364| /// distinct role is routed before derived-delivery planning.\n00365| fn names_deferred_work_product(content: &str) -> bool {\n00366|     let lower = content\n00367|         .trim()\n00368|         .trim_end_matches(['.', '!', '?', '。', '！', '？'])\n00369|         .trim_end()\n00370|         .to_lowercase();\n00371|     seed::lexicon()\n00372|         .role_word_forms(seed::ROLE_FILE_WRITE_DEFERRED_CONTENT_REFERENCE)\n00373|         .iter()\n00374|         .any(|form| match form.slot() {\n00375|             Slot::Bare => lower == form.text,\n00376|             Slot::Suffix => ends_with_head_noun(&lower, form.after_slot().trim_start()),\n00377|             Slot::Prefix | Slot::Circumfix => false,\n00378|         })\n00379| }\n00380| /// Whether `content` ends with `noun` standing as its own word.\n00381| ///\n00382| /// English and Russian separate a head noun from its modifiers with a space, so\n00383| /// the character before the match settles it. Chinese writes the same phrase\n00384| /// with no separator at all, which is why the boundary is stated as \"not an\n00385| /// ASCII alphanumeric\" rather than \"whitespace\": demanding a space would never\n00386| /// match 结论, and demanding nothing would match the tail of an unrelated\n00387| /// English word.\n00388| fn ends_with_head_noun(content: &str, noun: &str) -> bool {\n00389|     !noun.is_empty()\n00390|         && content.strip_suffix(noun).is_some_and(|before| {\n00391|             before\n00392|                 .chars()\n00393|                 .next_back()\n00394|                 .is_none_or(|character| !character.is_ascii_alphanumeric())\n00395|         })\n00396| }\n00397| \n\n(End of file - total 397 lines)\n</file>"
